    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d597">

                <pb facs="0702" n="606" sameAs="frus:frus1899/pg606"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Day to Sir Julian Pauncefote.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1898-07-07"><hi rendition="#italic">July 7,
                        1898</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1079.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: I have the honor to inclose for your
                    information a copy of a note<anchor n="1" xml:id="d597-f1"/> from the German
                    embassy, of the 7th instant, and of my reply of to-day’s date, assenting to the
                    return of the chiefs who were exiled with Mataafa, on condition that each were
                    willing to sign a protocol similar to that mentioned in the Department’s note,
                    No. 1066, of June 25, 1898, and which promised allegiance to the government of
                    Samoa. It is further stipulated, however, that the consent of Her Majesty’s
                    Government should first be given to this disposition of the incident.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">William R. Day.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
